{"componentChunkName":"component---src-templates-single-project-tsx","path":"/projects/bluetooth-directional-wayfinder","result":{"data":{"project":{"Completed":"2 years ago","dateCompleted":"2024-10-10","Title":"Bluetooth Directional Wayfinder","RepoLink":"https://github.com/Found-Design","LiveLink":"https://github.com/Found-Design","skills":[{"skill":{"Link":"https://reactnative.dev/","Mastery":"In Progress","SkillName":"React Native"},"SkillCategory":"frontend"},{"skill":{"Link":"https://www.raspberrypi.com/for-home/","Mastery":"Mastered","SkillName":"Raspberry Pi"},"SkillCategory":"creative"},{"skill":{"Link":"https://developer.apple.com/swift/","Mastery":"To Do","SkillName":"Swift"},"SkillCategory":"other"}],"Narrative":{"data":{"childMarkdownRemark":{"html":"<p>Hapytycs is an embedded haptic motor that syncs with your GPS signal and provides realtime turn by turn directional data.  Think of a bike helmet that vibrates left or right when a turn is coming.  Or handlebars that can vibrate when you made a wrong turn.</p>\n<p>The vision of haptycs is to fundamentally re-think how we interact with our environment through an new language.  I worked with an LA design firm to craft the application, system and hardware required for this visionary product.</p>\n<p>The project involved Arduino, raspberry pi and a great deal of learning how to write Swift applications that interface with the bluetooth and haptic motors.</p>"}}},"tags":[],"Cover":{"alternativeText":"haptycs-out.gif","localFile":{"url":"https://storage.googleapis.com/sigman-public-assets/haptycs_out_2149b7d80f/haptycs_out_2149b7d80f.gif","childImageSharp":null}},"Gallery":[{"localFile":{"childImageSharp":null}},{"localFile":{"childImageSharp":{"gatsbyImageData":{"layout":"fullWidth","backgroundColor":"#084848","images":{"fallback":{"src":"/static/ec9dff062967f967c488f2632a14a77c/bd89a/IMG_7975_bac82be659.png","srcSet":"/static/ec9dff062967f967c488f2632a14a77c/bd89a/IMG_7975_bac82be659.png 500w","sizes":"100v